  "gate": {
    "G1": {
      "verdict": "DISAGREE with blanket claim",
      "answer": "Not all six are decision-relevant. All three zero keys (exists, bytes=, none) are inert in the first census loop, which iterates c7_shape.items(); with seeding, each is 0 and C7_SHAPE_PIN.get(k)==v always holds. They can only fail in the second loop if the seeding line is deleted; the three nonzero keys are decision-relevant."
    },
    "G2": {
      "verdict": "DISAGREE",
      "answer": "Wrong for a plain dict: missing keys are absent from iteration, but accessing them via d[k] raises KeyError, while d.get(k) or `k not in d` handles them. Seeding is a convenience for iterating/checking all six uniformly, not something that makes absence checkable; `k not in c7_shape` already checks checkably."
    },
    "G3": {
      "verdict": "YES",
      "answer": "The census classifies only the first whitespace token of column 6 and never reads column 5 (the product path). Swapping/altering the product path or changing regex text/count annotation without changing the shape head keeps all six counts pinned, yet C7 live-checks or their meaning change; e.g. taking a `grep=` row pointing at R14_COMMON.md and repointing it at another staged file that happens to contain the same regex keeps census 24/3/4/0/0/0 but now checks a different product."
    },
    "G4": {
      "verdict": "NO",
      "answer": "The staged copy is not genuinely equivalent in general. derive_needed() spies io.open/builtins.open plus all top-level .py in R14, and misses paths reached only via os.path.getsize/isfile/exists (e.g. C5 LEDGER=lead/GOAL_THREE_TARGETS_20261018.md), modules loaded via importlib._io.open_code outside R14, and files whose paths are computed from data (C9 col-5 products such as _r94/falsify_r94.py if not io.open-spied in the baseline). NEEDED is derived from one baseline run: if a mutation changes which files the gate opens, those newly opened files are not staged."
    }
  }
}